2026-06-30 14:51:27,265 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-36f7e24a-09f5-4cd5-a623-01bb232be8d1', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nA201.raw\nA202.raw\nA203.raw\nA20TNF1.raw\nA20TNF2.raw\nA20TNF3.raw\nA20TNF4.raw\nA20TNF5.raw\nControl1.raw\nControl10.raw\nControl11.raw\nControl12.raw\nControl13.raw\nControl14.raw\nControl15.raw\nControl16.raw\nControl17.raw\nControl18.raw\nControl19CDK21.raw\nControl2.raw\nControl3.raw\nControl4.raw\nControl5.raw\nControl6.raw\nControl7.raw\nControl8.raw\nControl9.raw\nDMSO1.raw\nDMSO2.raw\nDMSO3.raw\nDMSO4.raw\nFADD1.raw\nFADD2.raw\nFADD3.raw\nFADD4.raw\nMYD881.raw\nMYD882.raw\nMYD883.raw\nNEMO1.raw\nNEMO2.raw\nNEMO3.raw\nNEMO4.raw\nRNF411.raw\nRNF412.raw\nRNF413.raw\nReversine1.raw\nReversine2.raw\nReversine3.raw\nReversine4.raw\nSimvastatin1.raw\nSimvastatin2.raw\nSimvastatin3.raw\nTANK1.raw\nTANK2.raw\nTANK3.raw\nTamoxifen1.raw\nTamoxifen2.raw\nTamoxifen3.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nVirotrap: Abducting Protein Complexes from Mammalian Cells\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nCell lysis is an inevitable step in classical affinity purification - mass spectrometry (AP-MS) strategies to map intracellular protein interactions. Cell homogenization implies a drastic change of the protein complex environment which strongly impedes comprehensive analysis. Complementary lysis conditions, in situ crosslinking strategies and proximal labeling techniques have been recently developed to address part of this problem. We here demonstrate the use of a viral particle sorting approach as an alternative lysis-free method. By fusing a bait protein to the HIV-1 GAG protein, we show that interaction partners of the bait protein become trapped within virus-like particles (VLPs) that bud from mammalian cells. This so-called Virotrap approach obviates the need for cell homogenization and preserves the protein complexes during purification. Using an efficient VLP enrichment protocol, Virotrap allows the detection of known binary interactions as well as MS-based identification of novel protein partners. In addition, we show the identification of stimulus-dependent interactions and demonstrate trapping of protein partners for small molecules. Virotrap constitutes an elegant complementary approach to the current arsenal of MS-based methods to study protein complexes.\n\n[METHODS]\nFor mass spectrometry, 107 HEK293T cells were seeded in 3-4 75 cm2 bottles and transfected the next day with a total of 15 \uf06dg DNA per bottle using polyethyleneimine (PEI) reagent. The following DNA/PEI quantities were used: GAG-bait 7.5 \uf06dg; mock vector 5.4 \uf06dg; 2.1 \uf06dg of a 1/2 pMD2.G - pcDNA3-FLAG-VSV-G mix vs. 37.5 \uf06dl PEI. The cellular supernatant was harvested after 32 h and centrifuged for 3 min at 450xg to remove cellular debris. For simvastatin, tamoxifen and reversin experiments, the bivalent methotrexate-polyethylene glycol-small molecules 20, 36 were added after transfection to the producing cells at a concentration of 1 \uf06dM.  Producer cells transfected with the A20 bait, VSV-G capture proteins and 1.15 \uf06dg TNFR plasmid were treated with 300 IU/ml of human TNF alpha during production to monitor dynamic A20 complexes.   The cleared supernatant was then filtered using 0.45 \uf06dm filters (Millipore). A total of 100 \uf06dl MyOne™ Streptavidin T1 beads pre-loaded with 10 \uf06dl ANTI-FLAG® BioM2-Biotin antibody was used to bind the tagged particles. Particles were allowed to bind for 2 h by end-over-end rotation. The total supernatant was processed in 3 consecutive binding steps.  Bead-particle complexes were washed once with washing buffer (20 mM Tris-HCl pH 7.5, 150 mM NaCl) and were then eluted with FLAG peptide (30 min at 37°C; 200 \uf06dg/ml in washing buffer) and lysed by addition of SDS to a final concentration of 0.1%. After 5 min, SDS was removed using  HiPPR Detergent Removal Spin Columns (Pierce, Thermo Scientific) followed by boiling and overnight digestion with 0.5 \uf06dg sequence-grade trypsin (Promega). After acidification (addition of 1 \uf06dl of 10% trifluoroacetic acid), the peptides were separated by nano-LC and directly analyzed with a Q Exactive instrument (Thermo Scientific) operating in MS/MS mode as described before\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
