2026-06-30 14:51:46,084 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-8563cf2d-2f2a-4468-8ef6-014733daf237', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to the mass spectrometry acquisition.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: instrument\nsdrf_column: comment[instrument]\nontology: MS:1000031 (PSI-MS)\ndescription: Mass spectrometer instrument make and model.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS ontology. If multiple instruments, return a list.\n  Common instruments (name → accession):\n    Orbitrap Astral → MS:1003356\n    Orbitrap Exploris 480 → MS:1003028\n    Orbitrap Eclipse → MS:1003349\n    Orbitrap Fusion Lumos → MS:1002732\n    Orbitrap Fusion → MS:1002416\n    Q Exactive HF-X → MS:1002877\n    Q Exactive HF → MS:1002523\n    Q Exactive Plus → MS:1002634\n    Q Exactive → MS:1001911\n    LTQ Orbitrap Velos → MS:1001742\n    LTQ Orbitrap XL → MS:1000556\n    timsTOF → MS:1003005\n    timsTOF Pro → MS:1003230\n    TripleTOF 6600 → MS:1002583\n  Format: NT=<instrument name>;AC=<MS accession>\n\nterm: proteomics data acquisition method\nsdrf_column: comment[proteomics data acquisition method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000659\ndescription: Mass spectrometry data acquisition method.\ninstructions: For XL-MS this is nearly always DDA. Use:\n  NT=data-dependent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000041 — for DDA/shotgun\n  NT=data-independent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000449 — for DIA (rare in XL-MS)\n  NT=targeted acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000450 — for SRM/PRM\n  Default to DDA if not stated.\n\nterm: dissociation method\nsdrf_column: comment[dissociation method]\nontology: MS:1000044 (PSI-MS children)\ndescription: Fragmentation method used for MS/MS.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS terms. If multiple methods, return a list.\n  Common methods:\n    HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422\n    CID → NT=CID;AC=MS:1002472\n    ETD → NT=ETD;AC=MS:1000598\n    EThcD → NT=EThcD;AC=MS:1002678\n    UVPD → NT=UVPD;AC=MS:1003247\n    stepped HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422 (stepped HCD is still HCD)\n\nterm: collision energy\nsdrf_column: comment[collision energy]\nontology: MS:1000045\ndescription: Collision energy used for fragmentation.\npattern: {value} {unit} where unit is NCE, %, or eV.\n  Stepped HCD: {value} {unit};{value} {unit};{value} {unit}\n  Examples: "30 NCE", "30% NCE", "27 eV", "25 NCE;27 NCE;30 NCE"\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: dia method\nsdrf_column: comment[dia method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000450\ndescription: Specific DIA method variant (only relevant for DIA datasets).\ninstructions: Return null for DDA XL-MS datasets (which is nearly all XL-MS).\n  If DIA is used, specify the variant (e.g. "SWATH", "MSe", "diaPASEF").\n\nterm: fragment mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[fragment mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000576\ndescription: Fragment ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "20 ppm", "0.02 Da")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical XL-MS values: 10–20 ppm for\n  Orbitrap instruments, 0.02–0.05 Da for ion trap.\n\nterm: precursor mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[precursor mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000575\ndescription: Precursor ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "10 ppm", "5 ppm")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical: 5–10 ppm.\n\nterm: technical replicate\nsdrf_column: comment[technical replicate]\nontology: MS:1001808\ndescription: Identifier for the technical replicate (integer).\ninstructions: Describe the technical replication strategy found in the text\n  (e.g. "2 technical replicates per sample", "no technical replicates stated").\n  Actual integers are assigned per-file automatically.\n\nterm: data file\nsdrf_column: comment[data file]\nontology: MS:1003083\ndescription: Name of the raw data file (.raw, .wiff, .d).\ninstructions: This is always auto-derived from the PRIDE file list.\n  Describe the file naming convention you observe (e.g. "sample_condition_rep.raw").\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nVirotrap: Abducting Protein Complexes from Mammalian Cells\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nCell lysis is an inevitable step in classical affinity purification - mass spectrometry (AP-MS) strategies to map intracellular protein interactions. Cell homogenization implies a drastic change of the protein complex environment which strongly impedes comprehensive analysis. Complementary lysis conditions, in situ crosslinking strategies and proximal labeling techniques have been recently developed to address part of this problem. We here demonstrate the use of a viral particle sorting approach as an alternative lysis-free method. By fusing a bait protein to the HIV-1 GAG protein, we show that interaction partners of the bait protein become trapped within virus-like particles (VLPs) that bud from mammalian cells. This so-called Virotrap approach obviates the need for cell homogenization and preserves the protein complexes during purification. Using an efficient VLP enrichment protocol, Virotrap allows the detection of known binary interactions as well as MS-based identification of novel protein partners. In addition, we show the identification of stimulus-dependent interactions and demonstrate trapping of protein partners for small molecules. Virotrap constitutes an elegant complementary approach to the current arsenal of MS-based methods to study protein complexes.\n\n[METHODS]\nFor mass spectrometry, 107 HEK293T cells were seeded in 3-4 75 cm2 bottles and transfected the next day with a total of 15 \uf06dg DNA per bottle using polyethyleneimine (PEI) reagent. The following DNA/PEI quantities were used: GAG-bait 7.5 \uf06dg; mock vector 5.4 \uf06dg; 2.1 \uf06dg of a 1/2 pMD2.G - pcDNA3-FLAG-VSV-G mix vs. 37.5 \uf06dl PEI. The cellular supernatant was harvested after 32 h and centrifuged for 3 min at 450xg to remove cellular debris. For simvastatin, tamoxifen and reversin experiments, the bivalent methotrexate-polyethylene glycol-small molecules 20, 36 were added after transfection to the producing cells at a concentration of 1 \uf06dM.  Producer cells transfected with the A20 bait, VSV-G capture proteins and 1.15 \uf06dg TNFR plasmid were treated with 300 IU/ml of human TNF alpha during production to monitor dynamic A20 complexes.   The cleared supernatant was then filtered using 0.45 \uf06dm filters (Millipore). A total of 100 \uf06dl MyOne™ Streptavidin T1 beads pre-loaded with 10 \uf06dl ANTI-FLAG® BioM2-Biotin antibody was used to bind the tagged particles. Particles were allowed to bind for 2 h by end-over-end rotation. The total supernatant was processed in 3 consecutive binding steps.  Bead-particle complexes were washed once with washing buffer (20 mM Tris-HCl pH 7.5, 150 mM NaCl) and were then eluted with FLAG peptide (30 min at 37°C; 200 \uf06dg/ml in washing buffer) and lysed by addition of SDS to a final concentration of 0.1%. After 5 min, SDS was removed using  HiPPR Detergent Removal Spin Columns (Pierce, Thermo Scientific) followed by boiling and overnight digestion with 0.5 \uf06dg sequence-grade trypsin (Promega). After acidification (addition of 1 \uf06dl of 10% trifluoroacetic acid), the peptides were separated by nano-LC and directly analyzed with a Q Exactive instrument (Thermo Scientific) operating in MS/MS mode as described before\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
